    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d162">

                <pb facs="0299" n="273" xml:id="pg273"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/5–1554: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Korea</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Briggs</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >secret<lb/>priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Seoul</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-05-15T16:00:00+08:30">May 15, 1954—4 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1182. Repeated information Tokyo 700, Geneva 83. Geneva for Smith; Tokyo pass
                    CINCUNC. For the Secretary from Dean. Reference Geneva 73<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d162-f1"/> and 74,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d162-f2"/> sent Department
                    196, Secto 200, Tokyo 57 and 58; also Department 923, repeated Geneva Tosec 164,
                    Tokyo 2532.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d162-f3"/> Ambassadors Briggs and Dean called
                    on President Rhee, Prime Minister and acting Foreign Minister at 1:30 p.m.
                    Stressed necessity all 15 nations on our side being able unite and present broad
                    statement of general principles and emphasized we could work out details later.
                    Further emphasized very important both world American and Congressional opinion
                    we stand together at Geneva in order be able fulfill our commitments elsewhere
                    in world and carry out appropriate overall survey ROK army now under discussion.
                    After going over Geneva 74 draft and suggested changes contained Department 923
                    explained, we prefer 74 text but that Secretary considered unanimous agreement
                    on general principles very important and so were prepared agree to these changes
                    if satisfactory to President. President read draft and riders carefully and we
                    explained general theory and emphasized ROK one of parties. Very little
                    discussion; President very amiable. Said would study and let us know. President
                    occupied all morning US Armed Forces Day. Do not believe they had as yet time
                    digest contents Pyun’s report transmitted Geneva 76.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d162-f4"/> In presenting matter stressed urgent desirability of
                    immediate agreement on principles so that they can be submitted Geneva
                    soonest.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Briggs</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
